{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018BZEzA5RfvfsBHaYdX8dr9","type":"tool_result","content":"}\n\n// Deps are the controller's collaborators.\ntype Deps struct {\n\tLog *slog.Logger\n\t// Config is called before every tick for a fresh snapshot, and once at\n\t// startup. An error counts as paused (fail closed): claiming loops are\n\t// skipped, and when no config has ever loaded no loop runs until one does.\n\tConfig func(ctx context.Context) (*config.Resolved, error)\n\tPause  Pauser\n\t// Startup hooks run in order before any loop starts; any error aborts Run.\n\tStartup []Hook\n\t// Grace is the shutdown wait; 0 means DefaultGrace.\n\tGrace time.Duration\n\t// Force is closed on a second signal; it abandons the grace wait.\n\tForce <-chan struct{}\n\t// Rand returns a value in [0,1); nil means math/rand/v2.Float64.\n\tRand func() float64\n}\n\n// Controller schedules loops.\ntype Controller struct {\n\tdeps  Deps\n\tloops []Loop\n\n\tinflight *Inflight // set by Run\n\n\tmu     sync.Mutex\n\tactive map[string]bool\n}\n\n// New validates deps and loop names. It returns an error for a nil Log,\n// Config or Pause, or an empty or duplicate loop name.\n\t\tif err := h.Fn(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"startup %s: %w\", h.Name, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tlog.Info(\"controller.hook\", \"name\", h.Name, \"duration\", time.Since(start).String())\n\t}\n\t// A config that cannot be loaded does not abort startup: the controller\n\t// starts paused, with no config, and retries on every tick.\n\tlctx, cancelLoad := context.WithTimeout(ctx, configTimeout)\n\tcfg, err := c.deps.Config(lctx)\n\tcancelLoad()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tlog.Error(\"config.initial_failed\", \"error\", err)\n\t\tcfg = nil\n\t}\n\n\t// Work contexts outlive the first signal; only grace expiry or Force\n\t// cancels them.\n\tworkCtx, cancelWork := context.WithCancel(context.WithoutCancel(ctx))\n\tdefer cancelWork()\n\tc.inflight = NewInflight(workCtx, log)\n\tstopping := make(chan struct{})\n\n\tnames := make([]string, len(c.loops))\n\tvar wg sync.WaitGroup\n\tfor i, l := range c.loops {\n\t\tnames[i] = l.Name()\n\t\tr := &runner{c: c, loop: l, log: log.With(\"loop\", l.Name()), cfg: cfg, cfgBroken: err != nil}\n\t\twg.Add(1)\n\t\tgo func() {\n\t\t\tdefer wg.Done()\n\t\t\tr.run(workCtx, stopping)\n\t\t}()\n\t}\n\tlog.Info(\"controller.start\", \"loops\", names)\n\n\t<-ctx.Done()\n\tlog.Info(\"controller.stopping\")\n\tclose(stopping)\n\n\tgrace := c.deps.Grace\n\tif grace == 0 {\n\t\tgrace = DefaultGrace\n\t}\n\ttimer := time.NewTimer(grace)\n\tdefer timer.Stop()\n\n\tdrained := make(chan struct{})\n\tgo func() {\n\t\twg.Wait() // every in-progress tick has returned\n\t\tc.inflight.close()\n\t\t<-c.inflight.Wait()\n\t\tclose(drained)\n\t}()\n\n\tvar result error\n\tselect {\n\tcase <-drained:\n\tcase <-timer.C:\n\t\tabandoned := append(c.activeTicks(), c.inflight.Pending()...)\n\t\tresult = fmt.Errorf(\"shutdown grace %s exceeded; abandoned: %s\", grace, strings.Join(abandoned, \", \"))\n\t\tc.abandon(cancelWork, drained)\n\tcase <-c.deps.Force:\n\t\tresult = errors.New(\"forced shutdown\")\n\t\tc.abandon(cancelWork, drained)\n\t}\n\tlog.Info(\"controller.stopped\", \"clean\", result == nil)\n21:// work context after the grace period expires or Force closes.\n42:\t// Force is closed on a second signal; it abandons the grace wait.\n43:\tForce <-chan struct{}\ndocs/runbooks/gate-2.md:87:interval (triage 15 min, workers and reviewers 5 min). Until #161 adds a wake\ndocs/runbooks/gate-2.md:88:signal, restarting `serve` when no job is running (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM jobs\ndocs/runbooks/gate-2.md:111:designed. Follow-ups: #156 (blocks stage B), #157 to #161.\ndocs/TODO.md:35:- #161 Wake the loops without restarting ard serve\ndocs/proposal.md:93:**Retries are explicit.** Every retry, automatic or human, is a new job with its own id (and attempt 1). A human retries a failed job by removing `ard:executor:failed`. The controller retries on its own once, only for a transient cause it can name: a rate limit, a clone failure, a controller restart (the job was orphaned, gone or never started across it), or node eviction under Kubernetes. The failed job then gets no `ard:executor:failed` and keeps its request label, so the next claim runs it again; that claim is linked to the failed job (`retry_of` in local state), so cost and causes can be traced. An automatic retry that fails again is marked failed. A usage-limit stop is not a failure: it closes out as `usage-limit`, with no `ard:executor:failed` and the request label kept, and it neither uses up nor grants the automatic retry. The re-run after the reset is a new job linked to the stopped one through `after_limit` in local state, which also carries `retry_of` over, so a limit stop of an automatic retry does not earn a second retry. Its comment says the issue is paused for the usage limit until a time. A throttle that the CLI says is \"not your usage limit\" is an ordinary rate-limit retry, not a usage-limit stop. Thresholds and early warnings are [#63](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/63).\ndocs/proposal.md:219:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper.\ndocs/proposal.md:256:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\ndocs/proposal.md:260:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected.\ndocs/proposal.md:266:| Heartbeat | PID alive plus log file mtime | pod phase plus container restarts |\ndocs/proposal.md:281:1. **Who acts.** The Reaper kills only a job that a goroutine in this process still drives (its pool slot is held and was not adopted at startup), and only for a deadline or a quiet heartbeat; it records the reason first, and the owner's close-out reads it. A worker keeps its job after the agent exits (tests, fix rounds), so \"exited\" alone never means abandoned. Any job nobody drives (adopted after a restart, or no slot) is closed out by the Reaper itself, and its adopted slot is released. A job whose close-out has started is resumed, never failed.\ndocs/proposal.md:321:6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) must re-verify that no code changed since `Plan.Review.CommitSHA` before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\ndocs/proposal.md:417:- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\ndocs/proposal.md:426:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs and the Reaper continue. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\nadopt.go\nadopt_test.go\ncfgloader.go\ncfgloader_test.go\nclone.go\nclone_test.go\ncommitters.go\nlimits_test.go\nreaping.go\nreaping_test.go\nrebuild.go\nrebuild_test.go\nreviewer.go\nreviewer_e2e_test.go\nscanners.go\nscanners_test.go\nserve.go\nserve_test.go\ntriage.go\ntriage_e2e_test.go\ntriage_test.go\nworker.go\nworker_e2e_test.go\n17:func TestFirstAndLaterTicks(t *testing.T) {\n41:func TestJitterBounds(t *testing.T) {\n62:func TestNoOverlapWhenTickIsSlow(t *testing.T) {\n83:func TestPanicIsRecovered(t *testing.T) {\n105:func TestBackoff(t *testing.T) {","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b568cb7-6c40-4f66-b76d-4b3bb13d5c28","uuid":"4da46d57-f962-49ff-a52f-f6a0d5a97489","timestamp":"2026-11-23T12:55:21.926Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"}\n\n// Deps are the controller's collaborators.\ntype Deps struct {\n\tLog *slog.Logger\n\t// Config is called before every tick for a fresh snapshot, and once at\n\t// startup. An error counts as paused (fail closed): claimi