`retry_of` in local state), so cost and causes can be traced. An automatic retry that fails again is marked failed. A usage-limit stop is not a failure: it closes out as `usage-limit`, with no `ard:executor:failed` and the request label kept, and it neither uses up nor grants the automatic retry. The re-run after the reset is a new job linked to the stopped one through `after_limit` in local state, which also carries `retry_of` over, so a limit stop of an automatic retry does not earn a second retry. Its comment says the issue is paused for the usage limit until a time. A throttle that the CLI says is \"not your usage limit\" is an ordinary rate-limit retry, not a usage-limit stop. Thresholds and early warnings are [#63](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/63).\ndocs/proposal.md:219:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is