ookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\ndocs/proposal.md:260:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected.\ndocs/proposal.md:266:| Heartbeat | PID alive plus log file mtime | pod phase plus container restarts |\ndocs/proposal.md:281:1. **Who acts.** The Reaper kills only a job that a goroutine in this process still drives (its pool slot is held and was not adopted at startup), and only for a deadline or a quiet heartbeat; it records the reason first, and the owner's close-out reads it. A worker keeps its job after the agent exits (tests, fix rounds), so \"exited\" alone never means abandoned. Any job nobody drives (adopted after a restart, or no slot) is closed out by the Reaper itself, and its adopted slot is released. A job whose close-out has started is resumed, never failed.\ndocs/proposal.md:321:6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) must re-verify that no code changed since `Plan.Review.CommitSHA` before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\ndocs/proposal.md:417:- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\ndocs/proposal.md:426:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs and the Reaper continue. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\nadopt.go\nadopt_test.go\ncfgloader.go\ncfgloader_test.go\nclone.go\nclone_test.go\ncommitters.go\nlimits_test.go\nreaping.go\nreaping_test.go\nrebuild.go\nrebuild_test.go\nreviewer.go\nreviewer_e2e_test.go\nscanners.go\nscanners_test.go\nserve.go\nserve_test.go\ntriage.go\ntriage_e2e_test.go\ntriage_test.go\nworker.go\nworker_e2e_test.go\n17:func TestFirstAndLaterTicks(t *testing.T) {\n41:func TestJitterBounds(t *testing.T) {\n62:func TestNoOverlapWhenTickIsSlow(t *testing.T) {\n83:func TestPanicIsRecovered(t *testing.T) {\n105:func TestBackoff(t *testing.T) {","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018BZEzA5RfvfsBHaYdX8dr9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
